  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1242 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nThursday through early next week (Tuesday)...\n\nThursday into weekend: A change to a wetter weather pattern is \nstill anticipated for late this week into the weekend as the \npreviously mentioned trough helps to induce increased southerly \nflow and moisture transport, with deep level moisture eventually \nincreasing to anomalous levels over the forecast area (e.g., \nprecipitable water 2 to 3 SD positive anomalies). Given the \nprolonged presence of weak synoptic scale ascent related to the \nsubtle but persistent upper jet influence and modest low level \nmoisture convergence, the forecast reflects a long duration of \nhigh rain chances in the Friday through Sunday time frame. \n\nWhile prospects for a wet weekend remain high, the potential impacts\nrelated to this upcoming wet weather pattern are quite uncertain,\nespecially when considering that model guidance has continued to \nshift weaker with the upper trough presence and stronger with the \nsoutheast CONUS subtropical high center. Because of this, heavier \nQPF has shifted some closer to the coast and brings into question \nthe potential for heavier rainfall. Regardless of the heavy rain\npotential, given how dry it has been recently, the rain that does\nfall would be highly beneficial in most cases. \n\nNext week: Looking ahead, drier and somewhat cooler air should \nsurge into the forecast area as northwest flow aloft becomes the \nprimary driver for our weather. /EC/",
